  "diagnosis": {
    "rootCause": "RuntimeBackend::infer converted every four-byte input chunk with f32::from_le_bytes and collected a new 1,228,800-element Vec inside each timed candidate inference. The Legacy comparator borrowed the already prepared canonical input as &[f32], so the candidate alone paid approximately 50 ms of dev-profile decoding overhead. After removing that allocation, the behavior-required 705,600-value finite-output scan was the remaining unstable warm-p95 cost; an eight-lane unrolled scan reduced its Ubuntu dev-profile microbenchmark from approximately 2.09 ms to 0.80 ms without removing validation.",
    "repair": [
      "Borrow aligned f32 input bytes on little-endian targets through bytemuck::try_cast_slice.",
      "Retain the original little-endian byte decoding as the fallback for unaligned input and for big-endian targets.",
      "Retain finite-output rejection while reducing iterator overhead with an eight-value unrolled scan and remainder check."
    ],
